  "subtitle": "a programmable toy tank that taught a generation the gulf between typed commands and physical consequences",
  "why_scout_cares": "Big Trak is one of the earliest consumer devices to put procedural programming — not coding on a screen, but button-press sequencing executed in physical space — directly into the hands of children. No display, no compiler, no external computer. You typed FORWARD 5, RIGHT 15, FIRE on a membrane keypad, pressed GO, and the tank carried out your orders on the living room floor. The gap between intention and execution was measured in body-lengths and degrees of rotation, and the feedback was entirely physical: either the tank got where you meant it to go, or it rammed the coffee table. It is also one of the few artifacts in the museum that was adopted as a research platform by cognitive scientists — David Klahr's lab at Carnegie Mellon used Big Trak to study how people learn without instructions, making it simultaneously a toy, a robot, and an instrument of scientific discovery.",
